From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 03:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] EXPERIMENT: misc: h713: hold bus-r-cpucfg and read the HPD register

0x07091014 is the HPD pin register -- three bits, 0x00 all ports low, 0x07 all
high -- and it is the last thing standing between this board and a source
seeing it as a display. Reading it from Linux hard-locked the SoC: serial
silent, network gone, power cycle to recover.

The cause was found by reading rather than probing, since that block had cost a
power cycle already. Neither device tree describes 0x07091000; stock declares
only rtc@7090000 and rtc_ccu@7090000, both 0x3ff long, so stock's Linux does
not describe it either, which fits the block being ARISC-private and driven by
firmware. The R-domain CCU does expose a gate for the block that owns the
region:

  r-ahb         en=1     r-apb0        en=3      bus-r-ppu    en=1
  bus-r-cpucfg  en=0     bus-r-timer   en=0      bus-r-twd    en=0

R_CPUCFG is the ARISC/CPUS configuration block. Its gate is off while the R-bus
fabric around it is up, so the fabric is not the problem and only the leaf is --
exactly the shape of the TVFE/TVCAP hang that 0087 fixed by claiming the gate.

So claim CLK_BUS_R_CPUCFG in the same minimal device and read the register from
inside the driver, with a flushed line naming the access immediately before it.
If the board stops there, the clock was not the cause and the last line says so.

This is a hypothesis under test, not a fix. bus-r-rtc is also en=0 while the RTC
works perfectly, because the RTC sits in an always-on domain -- so a gated bus
clock does not universally imply an inaccessible block, and this could still
hang.

Deliberately reads only. If it reads, writing 0x07 asserts HPD on all three
ports and the host GPU becomes a real instrument for every EDID question this
round had to answer by inference -- but that is a separate change, because a
write on top of an unproven read would make a hang ambiguous.

OUT OF SERIES. Applies after 0089.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c b/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c
--- a/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/h713-tvcap-probe.c
@@ -154,6 +154,49 @@
 	dev_info(tv->dev, "%s: readable\n", what);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The HPD pin register, and the last unexplained hard-lock on this board.
+ *
+ * 0x07091000 is described by no node in either our DT or stock's -- stock
+ * declares only rtc@7090000 and rtc_ccu@7090000, both 0x3ff long -- which fits
+ * the block being ARISC-private and driven by firmware. A read of it from
+ * Linux took the whole SoC down: serial silent, network gone, power cycle to
+ * recover.
+ *
+ * The candidate cause is CLK_BUS_R_CPUCFG, the gate for the ARISC/CPUS config
+ * block that owns this region. It reads en=0 at boot while r-ahb and r-apb0
+ * are up, so the R-bus fabric is alive and only the leaf is gated -- exactly
+ * the shape of the TVFE/TVCAP hang that patch 0087 fixed by claiming the gate.
+ *
+ * The clock is now held by this device, so this is that hypothesis under test.
+ * It is only a hypothesis: bus-r-rtc is also en=0 while the RTC works fine,
+ * because the RTC sits in an always-on domain, so a gated bus clock does not
+ * universally imply an inaccessible block.
+ */
+#define H713_HPD_BASE			0x07091000
+#define H713_HPD_SIZE			0x100
+#define H713_HPD_PORT_MASK		0x14
+
+static void h713_probe_hpd(struct h713_tvcap *tv)
+{
+	void __iomem *p;
+
+	p = devm_ioremap(tv->dev, H713_HPD_BASE, H713_HPD_SIZE);
+	if (!p) {
+		dev_err(tv->dev, "hpd: ioremap failed\n");
+		return;
+	}
+
+	dev_info(tv->dev, "hpd: about to READ 0x%08x with bus-r-cpucfg held -- if this is the last line, the clock was not the cause\n",
+		 H713_HPD_BASE + H713_HPD_PORT_MASK);
+
+	dev_info(tv->dev, "hpd: 0x%08x = 0x%08x\n",
+		 H713_HPD_BASE + H713_HPD_PORT_MASK,
+		 readl(p + H713_HPD_PORT_MASK));
+
+	dev_info(tv->dev, "hpd: readable\n");
+}
+
 static void h713_probe_wrapper(struct h713_tvcap *tv)
 {
 	/* Port-select lives at +0x8F1, so read the 0x800 neighbourhood. */
@@ -230,6 +273,7 @@
 		 readl(tv->thdmirx + THDMIRX_DMA_CONFIG11));
 
 	h713_probe_wrapper(tv);
+	h713_probe_hpd(tv);
 
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, tv);
 	return 0;
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -1587,9 +1587,18 @@
 			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_TVCAP>,
 				 <&ccu CLK_BUS_CAP_300M>,
 				 <&ccu CLK_VINCAP_DMA>,
-				 <&ccu CLK_TVFE_1296M>;
+				 <&ccu CLK_TVFE_1296M>,
+				 /*
+				  * R_CPUCFG is the ARISC/CPUS config block, and
+				  * 0x07091000 -- the HPD pin register -- lives
+				  * behind it. Its gate reads en=0 at boot while
+				  * r-ahb and r-apb0 are up, so the fabric is
+				  * alive and only this leaf is off, which is the
+				  * same shape as the TVFE/TVCAP hang.
+				  */
+				 <&r_ccu CLK_BUS_R_CPUCFG>;
 			clock-names = "bus_tvcap", "cap_300m", "vincap_dma",
-				      "tvfe_1296m";
+				      "tvfe_1296m", "r_cpucfg";
 			power-domains = <&ppu 1>, <&ppu 2>;
 			power-domain-names = "tvfe", "tvcap";
 			status = "okay";
